--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STEP 3 — Configure traffic to send across the stack                      PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3000 MAC addresses, 1500 per side, bidirectional (the 38393 pcaps):
  side A out eth1 : src 02:00:0a:00:00:01 .. 02:00:0a:00:05:dc
  side B out eth2 : src 02:00:0b:00:00:01 .. 02:00:0b:00:05:dc
  A[i] addresses B[i] and vice versa, so all frames are known-unicast.
  tcpreplay -i eth1 --pps=1000 --loop=0 fdb-a.pcap
  tcpreplay -i eth2 --pps=1000 --loop=0 fdb-b.pcap

Counted by SOURCE MAC (see CAVEAT 1):
  eth1: side-A 12084 frames / 1500 MACs (own TX)   side-B 12052 / 1500 (received)
  eth2: side-A 12946 frames / 1500 MACs (received) side-B 12914 / 1500 (own TX)

Forwarding confirmed in both directions, all 1500 distinct MACs per side.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STEP 4 — Reboot the stack master, wait for reform.
         Expected: traffic should continue to forward                    PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Master at the time was member 2. `reboot stack-member 2` issued at epoch
1787093377.611 with traffic running and tcpdump capturing both ends.

  A->B  eth1 -> AR4050 -> DUT -> x230 -> eth2
        248448 frames received over 249.3 s
        largest gap  0.901 s  at +2.063 s relative to reboot
  B->A  eth2 -> x230 -> DUT -> AR4050 -> eth1
        266342 frames received over 266.3 s
        largest gap  0.002 s  at +220.567 s   <- essentially uninterrupted


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STEP 5 — Shut down ports in the LACP aggregator to force traffic across the
         stack link. Expected: traffic should continue to forward        PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Shut `port2.0.9` (po1's member-2 leg) and `port1.0.9` (po2's member-1 leg) at
epoch 1787093729.719, leaving:

  po1 = port1.0.1  (member 1) only
  po2 = port2.0.1  (member 2) only

so ingress and egress are on DIFFERENT members and EVERY frame must traverse the
stack link. Confirmed by `show etherchannel summary`:
  po1 : port1.0.1 synchronized , port2.0.9 disabled
  po2 : port1.0.9 disabled     , port2.0.1 synchronized

Stack link `port1.0.28` counters over the following ~20 s:
  input  354299 -> 379488   = +25189   (~1.26k pps)
  output 372214 -> 397519   = +25305   (~1.27k pps)
matching the ~1000 pps per direction offered load — proof the traffic really is
transiting the stack link and not taking some other path.

Far-end delivery:
  A->B : 74975 frames after the shutdown, sustained  989 pps, gap 0.842 s
  B->A : 81910 frames after the shutdown, sustained  990 pps, gap 0.808 s

TRAFFIC CONTINUED TO FORWARD at full rate in both directions across the stack
link, after a sub-second reconvergence.

